Day#14 - Spatial Anchors in MR

Introduction--
--fixed digital reference points that lock virtual 3D objects to precise physical locations in the real world.
--They ensure augmented and mixed reality content stays stable, persists across different app sessions, and lines up correctly for multiple users.

How They Work--
--Sensor scanning:Devices use cameras and depth sensors to map room features like edges, corners, and surfaces.
--World locking:A coordinate system (position and rotation) ties the virtual object to those real-world features.
--Drift correction:The device constantly tracks changes in the environment to keep the digital item locked in place over time.

Core Benefits--
--Persistence: Leaving a virtual object or game setup on a real table and finding it in the exact same spot days later.
--Shared spaces: Allowing multiple people in the same room to see and interact with the same digital objects through their own headsets.
--Navigation: Placing virtual signs, notes, or characters that stay fixed to specific walls or pieces of furniture.

Real World Exmaple--
--Apple Vision Pro
-Apps can remember where virtual windows were placed in a room and restore them later.
--Microsoft HoloLens 2
-Engineers can place persistent holographic instructions on machinery that remain in the correct location across sessions.
--Meta Quest 3
-Mixed reality applications can anchor virtual objects to furniture and walls so they stay in place while the user moves around.

